UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'GAP-028 IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT 1:12 WAS MEASURED AND FOUND WANTING BEFORE IT WAS ADOPTED. GAP-028 asked whether the 1:12 threshold is anchored in measurement. REF-01005 (Steinfeld, Schroeder and Bishop 1979, HUD-PDR 397, ERIC ED184280, full text retrieved and persisted this batch) settles it. (1) THE MEASUREMENT EXISTS. A purpose-built adjustable 40-foot ramp, 124 disabled participants, slope stepped 1:12 then 1:16 then 1:20, success judged on distance, time and pulse recovery to within ten beats of resting. (2) IT FOUND 1:12 INADEQUATE: almost half the wheelchair users could not negotiate the full length at 1:12 and about a third could not travel even 5 feet; every wheelchair user completed 40 feet at 1:20, though only three reached that trial. (3) THE STUDY WAS COMMISSIONED TO IMPROVE THE VERY STANDARD THAT CARRIES 1:12 — its Foreword says the six-report series was sponsored to make buildings accessible "through improving the American National Standards Institute A117 standard". (4) ITS OWN RECOMMENDATION TABLE RESTS PARTLY ON OTHERS: Table 13 footnote c reads "Based on research of others (Templer, 1977 and Walter, 1971)", so the chain has three nodes and this project holds one. (5) WALTER 1971 AND STEINFELD 1979 CONVERGE at 1:16 over 20 feet for at least 95 percent of wheelchair users, per page 163 — held on Steinfeld testimony alone, so it is ONE document counted once until Walter is retrieved. WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not supply a current anchor. Owner ruling 2026-09-18 places 1979 work as historical grounding rather than a present standard, so REF-01005 is admitted T3 grey and parameter 3 x MOB stays undetermined. WHAT IS NOW OWED: retrieve Walter 1971 (candidate 109, record verified) and Templer 1977 (candidate 115) — the two remaining nodes of the chain. Derive the state rather than quoting this: select disposition, title from search_candidates where candidate_id in (109,110,111,115). || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: BATCH 19 WORKED BOTH OWED NODES. NEITHER IS RETRIEVED; BOTH ARE MATERIALLY NARROWED, AND ONE CLAUSE OF THIS GAP NEEDS QUALIFYING. (1) THE QUALIFICATION, WHICH RUNS AGAINST THIS GAP''S OWN FRAMING: clause (4) says Table 13 ''rests partly on others'' and treats Templer and Walter as the two remaining nodes of THE 1:12 CHAIN. The footnote marker c appears on the 1:8 and 1:10 rows ONLY, not on 1:12 or 1:16 -- derive it at pypdf page index 56, and note it is coherent rather than noise because Table 12 shows the study tested exactly 1:12, 1:16 and 1:20, so the warranted bands are precisely the two OUTSIDE its tested range. On that reading the named warrant covers the permissive end of the table and the 1:12 and 1:16 rows rest on Steinfeld''s own data, which means Templer and Walter are not nodes of the 1:12 chain at all. Recorded as extraction 56 and held as A READING, NOT AS SETTLED: these are single characters on a damaged scan and a cleaner copy of page 57 decides it. (2) TEMPLER: ''Templer, 1977'' does not resolve to any indexed item, and John A. Templer''s corpus is stairs end to end -- 1974 dissertation (the one this bibliography actually lists), 1978 NBS IR 78-1554 ''An analysis of the behavior of stair users'', 1984 ''The Forgiving Stair'', 1989 ''The Soft Stair''. Four items, fifteen years, no ramp study, nothing dated 1977. ERIC author:Templer returns 33 records with none architectural and ERIC Templer+stairs returns 0, which is a well-formed negative rather than a query-shape failure. Candidate 115''s title over-claimed it as ''the ramp-slope research'' and is corrected. Candidate 117 stages the 1978 NBS report as the nearest RESOLVABLE neighbour, explicitly without claiming it is the referent. (3) WALTER: the year 1971 is now corroborated by a Library of Congress MARC record, which matters because this bibliography''s own year digits for Walter are OCR-damaged and unreadable. The route this gap and candidate 109 both named next -- Jisc Library Hub Discover -- IS CLOSED: HTTP 403 behind a Cloudflare interstitial, twice. What batch 19 adds instead is the holdings layer that makes document delivery actionable: OCLC 222208, LCCN 77502052, ISBN-10 0901908053, LC class NA2545.P5 D5. (4) CLAUSE (5) OF THIS GAP STANDS UNCHANGED and should not be read as weakened: the 1:16 convergence is still ONE document counted once, on Steinfeld''s testimony about Walter, until Walter itself is read. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: BATCH 19 SECOND CORRECTION, RETRACTING MOST OF ITS FIRST. An adversarial pass over the same batch refuted the clause this gap gained hours earlier, and the retraction is larger than the claim. (1) RETRACTED: clause (2) of my earlier amendment said "Templer 1977 does not resolve to any indexed item, and John A. Templer corpus is stairs end to end ... Four items, fifteen years, no ramp study". FALSE. Templer, John A., Provisions for elderly and handicapped pedestrians, FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3, Georgia Tech Pedestrian Research Laboratory, is substantial RAMP research: Volume 3 opens Part I with "A Study of Short Ramps", carries 32 gradient-rating tables for manual and electric wheelchair users and cane users, and closes on TABLE 20 "Ramp Gradient Recommendations" over gradients 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16. Full text is free on Internet Archive -- the endpoint this same batch used for Walter an hour earlier and never pointed at Templer. Staged as candidate 125. The Crossref query I called a corpus resolution contained the word "stair", so it returned stair papers; that is query shape, which is R14, which the same note invoked against ERIC while exempting itself. (2) THE PRIORITY INFERENCE IS INVERTED, NOT MERELY WEAKENED. I wrote that the footnote scope means "Templer and Walter are not nodes of the 1:12 chain at all" and that the priority of retrieving them drops. Templer TABLE 20 recommends over 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16 -- THE SAME FOUR BANDS as Steinfeld Table 13, whose footnote c marks exactly the two Steinfeld did not test. Retrieving Templer is now the strongest available lead on what footnote c means, not a deprioritised one. (3) THE FOOTNOTE-SCOPE READING ITSELF SURVIVES and was confirmed independently on a rendered page image: the marker sits on 1:8 and 1:10 and not on 1:12 or 1:16, and Table 12 confirms the study stepped 1:12, 1:16, 1:20 only. But TWO THINGS BUILT ON IT DO NOT. The footnote reads "Walters, 1971", not "Walter" -- extraction 56 normalised that to Walter and blamed OCR damage, erasing a real internal inconsistency in the report between its footnote and its bibliography. And my inference that "the 1:12 and 1:16 rows rest on Steinfeld own data" over-reads an absent marker: the report own comparison section says "Walter findings for ramps are similar to ours. Both studies found a 1:16 slope for 20 feet to be accessible to at least 95 percent of the wheelchair users", so Walter IS corroboration for the 1:16 row by the report own words -- which is clause (5) of this gap, the clause my amendment said stood unchanged while contradicting it. (4) ALSO CORRECTED: "a cleaner copy of page 57 decides it in one look" -- the copy this project already holds decided it; nobody had looked. And page 57 is the 1-based PDF page; the PRINTED page is 54. A reader with the physical report was sent to the wrong page, and extraction 57 carries the same confusion (loc_section 163 is the PDF page, printed 161). (5) CLAUSE (5) OF THE ORIGINAL GAP STANDS, and now with a second reader: FHWA-RD-79-3 literature review independently describes Walter 1971 adjustable ramp -- 20 feet at 1:8.6 to 1:16, 10 feet at 1:5.5 to 1:10 -- so a retrievable document corroborates Steinfeld account of Walter. That is not Walter itself and does not make the convergence two documents.', "updated_at" = '2026-09-20 04:10' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-037';
UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'THE DISCOVERY-CATALOGUE STRATUM IS CLOSED IN THIS ENVIRONMENT, AND THREE BATCHES HAVE NOW EACH DISCOVERED IT SEPARATELY. Measured, all persisted as artefacts: catalog.hathitrust.org 403 + Cloudflare interstitial (batch 18); discover.libraryhub.jisc.ac.uk 403 + Cloudflare interstitial, twice (batch 19); trid.trb.org HTTP 200 serving a JS shell with no extractable record, although the page reports 20 results and the string Templer occurs in it five times (batch 19). NONE OF THESE IS A NULL RESULT. Nothing whatever is established about what HathiTrust, Jisc or TRID hold, and a later batch must not cite these as evidence of absence. WHAT THE PATTERN MEANS FOR PLANNING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF FILING IT: the pre-1990 layer this project keeps reaching for lives in library catalogues and technical-report indexes, and that is precisely the stratum this environment cannot see. Batches 17, 18 and 19 each independently built a retrieval ladder, each hit the same wall in a different place, and each recorded it locally. THE ROUTES THAT DO WORK, derived from the same three batches, are DEPOSIT-BASED rather than catalogue-based: ERIC (holds what was deposited in it -- it served ED184280 in full), Crossref (holds what publishers deposited), Open Library and Internet Archive (hold what was digitised), OpenAlex and CORE intermittently on a shared-IP daily budget. GOOGLE BOOKS IS NOT MERELY EXHAUSTED AND THE DISTINCTION MATTERS: batch 18 read its 429 as a daily quota and GAP-040 records it that way; queried again two days later the body reads quota_limit_value ''0'', a ZERO allocation for this project. It will not reset and no batch should spend a query discovering that again. ONE QUERY-SHAPE LESSON WORTH MORE THAN THE WHOLE LADDER: batch 18 searched Open Library for Walter 1971 BY TITLE and got 0; batch 19 searched the same index BY PUBLISHER, publisher:''Disabled Living Foundation'', and got 99 records with the target among them. Same index, same day''s coverage, opposite result. R14 says a zero-yield search is evidence of absence only if the query was well-formed, and for an institutional report the well-formed query is often the PUBLISHER, not the title -- an institutional report''s title is unstable across catalogues while its issuing body is not. WHAT IS OWED: this is a planning fact, not a defect to fix. The action it implies is that Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 should be handed to document delivery -- OCLC 222208 and LCCN 77502052 for Walter; ProQuest Dissertations or the University of Illinois repository for Elmer -- rather than to a fourth retrieval ladder. GAP-026 already said this in general terms one batch ago; this gap gives it the identifiers and the negative evidence that make it actionable. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: THREE CORRECTIONS TO THIS GAP, filed hours after it was, by an adversarial pass over the same batch. (1) THE TRID RESULT COUNT IS FABRICATED. This gap says TRID served "a JS shell ... although the page reports 20 results and the string Templer occurs in it five times". THE PERSISTED ARTEFACT CONTAINS NO RESULT COUNT AT ALL. The "20Results" I reported came from a regex hitting page furniture; the only results text in the body is "No results found." inside a hidden template div, and the five Templer occurrences are all echoes of my own query string -- two analytics meta tags, the search input value, a hidden searchFriendlyText and a mailto error-report body. I typed a number that the bytes do not support, inside a gap whose subject is people mis-scoring retrievals. Derive: grep the artefact for a result count and find none. (2) TRID IS REACHABLE AND THIS GAP FILES IT AS CLOSED. Batch 16 retrieved trid.trb.org/View/921388 -- HTTP 200, about 50 KB, a full record with abstract -- and it is in the retrieval log. Only the SEARCH UI is JS-rendered. Listing TRID beside HathiTrust and Jisc as "the discovery-catalogue stratum is closed" is wrong: a record page fetches fine, and a session that needs a TRID record should fetch it by id rather than conclude the host is shut. (3) THE CENTRAL CLAIM IS TOO STRONG AND THE SAME BATCH DISPROVED IT. This gap says the pre-1990 layer "lives in library catalogues and technical-report indexes, and that is precisely the stratum this environment cannot see". Templer FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3 and NBS IR 78-1554 are BOTH on Internet Archive, in full text, free -- and Internet Archive is on this gap own list of routes that work. The honest statement is narrower: the CATALOGUE HOSTS are Cloudflare-gated, and a federal technical report is often reachable anyway through a deposit-based archive. The planning conclusion changes with it: before handing a pre-1990 report to document delivery, search Internet Archive BY CREATOR, which this batch did for Walter and never did for Templer. WHAT SURVIVES: HathiTrust and Jisc really are Cloudflare-gated, both persisted; Google Books really does report quota_limit_value 0 rather than a resetting quota; and the publisher-versus-title query-shape lesson stands and is the most useful thing in this gap.', "updated_at" = '2026-09-20 04:10' WHERE "gap_id" = 'GAP-041';

