UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'THREE OF FOUR PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS FALSIFIED, AND THE REASONING BEHIND THE SURVIVING ONE WAS ALSO WRONG. Derive every figure here: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-01005 --compare. P1a FALSIFIED -- 38 references, not the 60-plus predicted. P1b FALSIFIED -- slope-strict scores 1-2, below the predicted 3-12 band. NOTE AGAINST MYSELF: my own pre-registered falsification condition said ''falsified if 0 or >=20'', which 1-2 does not trigger. The point prediction failed and the threshold I set to catch it was too lax to fire. Recording both rather than hiding behind the looser test. P1c FALSIFIED -- slope-loose scores 1-3 against slope-strict''s 1-2, nowhere near 3x. THE REASON I GAVE WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH KEEPING: I predicted the bare stem accessib would fire on everything because the report is an accessibility report. It fires on almost nothing, because the BIBLIOGRAPHY is not an accessibility bibliography -- it is a HUMAN FACTORS AND ERGONOMICS bibliography (Damon, Diffrient, Kroemer, McCormick, Murrell, Poulton, Human Factors and Applied Ergonomics as the recurring venues). A 1979 federal accessibility report drew its authority from ergonomics, not from a disability literature, and that is a finding about the period rather than about the screen. P1d CONFIRMED BUT WITH A DISCREPANCY THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE PREDICTION: both appear, and the Templer entry is 1974 on STAIR SHAPE, while Table 13 footnote c cites ''Templer, 1977''. Templer 1977 IS NOT IN THIS BIBLIOGRAPHY. THE YIELD IS A FLOOR, NOT A COUNT: the OCR is damaged, so an entry whose title is mangled below the screen''s terms is a false negative, and the band''s ceiling says how much could be hidden. TWO SLOPE-TITLED ENTRIES: Elmer C.D. (candidate 110, scores in the conservative band -- ''ramp'' is legible) and Corlett et al. ''Ramps or stairs'' in Applied Ergonomics 3 (ceiling only -- ''stairs'' is legible, ''Ramps'' is not, newly staged this pass). WALTER DOES NOT SCORE AT ALL, and that is the most instructive result: it is the single most slope-relevant item in the list, and its title in this bibliography carries no slope term because ''Part 3: Ramp gradients'' is a part title. A title screen cannot see it. Recorded so no later reader reads a low yield as a thin literature. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: THE PREMISE OF THIS PASS WAS FALSE AND THE YIELD IT REPORTED WAS WRONG. Found by an adversarial pass over this same batch. This search recorded that the ED184280 scan was unreadable -- "no OCR engine is available in this environment (no tesseract, no pdftotext), so this text layer is the best that exists here" -- and transcribed 38 entries with authors marked illegible, years marked unresolvable and one slope term marked absent. THE PAGES ARE JBIG2 IMAGES INSIDE THE PDF AND THEY RENDER LEGIBLY IN ABOUT A SECOND. pypdf reads a TEXT LAYER; when that layer is mush it says the layer is mush and nothing whatever about the document. I read the mush as a property of the page and then reasoned at length, carefully, and wrongly about the consequences. WHAT THE PAGES ACTUALLY SAY, re-transcribed this batch from renders at 130 dpi and persisted as the superseding artefact c9361532746ffec9.json: Birren J.E. Psychology of aging 1964 (recorded as "C[u]rren" and filed at alphabetical position C, which then required a bogus note claiming Brattgard was out of order -- the list is in strict order and the misreading invented the anomaly); Corlett, Hutcheson, DeLugan and Rogozenski, RAMPS OR STAIRS, Applied ergonomics 3:4 pp 195-201 1972 (the word Ramps was called illegible); Elmer C.D. ... University of IL, 1957 (the year was called unresolvable); Jones J.C. Fitting for action (recorded as AUTHOR LOST TO THE SCAN); Kidwell A.M.; Kira A.; Nichols P.J.R.; Harrigan J.E.; Leonard J.A.; Steinfeld, EDWARD (the OCR read Eckard and I recorded Eckard as the reading); Templer J.A. ... New York: COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY 1974; Walter, F. ... 1971 (I called the initial NOT an F on its face and the year OCR-damaged, and staged a candidate partly to chase the unexplained initial -- there was nothing to explain). THE YIELD IS CORRECTED FROM A BAND TO A POINT: slope-strict is 2, not the 1-2 reported, because Corlett scores in the floor once the title is read rather than guessed. Re-derive: python3 scripts/research/mining_screen.py --ref REF-01005 --compare, which now reports provenance READ rather than SCRAPED. WHAT SURVIVES UNCHANGED: 38 entries; the ANSI-to-Webb alphabetical span is complete; Walter still carries no slope term in its title, so the title-screen false negative recorded for it stands and is the pass real finding. THE MECHANISM IS FIXED, not just the reading: scripts/research/page_image.py renders a persisted PDF page so a session can look at the scan, pymupdf is declared in the check registry batteries, and mining_screen now distinguishes READ from SCRAPED and lets a superseding transcription win over the one it replaces.' WHERE "exec_id" = 88;
UPDATE "search_executions" SET "findings_note" = 'TEMPLER 1977 DOES NOT RESOLVE TO ANY INDEXED ITEM, AND THE SURNAME IS NOW SETTLED AS John A. Templer, A STAIRS RESEARCHER. THE NEGATIVE IS WELL-FORMED, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT USABLE (R14). ERIC author:\"Templer\" returns 33 records -- a healthy result set, so the query shape is sound -- and NOT ONE is architectural: they are Bill Templer (education/politics), Lois Templer, Andrew J. Templer (management), Donald I. Templer (psychology, death-anxiety scales), Sally Templer. ERIC \"Templer\" AND \"stairs\" returns 0. A well-formed author query returning 33 and a topic conjunction returning 0 is GENUINE ABSENCE IN ERIC, not a query-shape failure -- and ERIC being an education index, absence there says nothing about the built-environment literature. P3b CORRECT AT 0.2. OpenAlex raw_author_name Templer + year 1977 returns 10, all of them Jerry W. Templer (ENT surgery), Donald I. Templer (psychology), Andrew J. Templer (management) or J.C. Templer. NO J.A. TEMPLER IN 1977 IN EITHER INDEX. P3a FALSIFIED: TRID served a 200 with a results page, and the page does not carry a single /View/ record id -- the markup is JS-rendered, so what came back is a shell. ''Templer'' appears 5 times in the body but no record is extractable. NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT TRID HOLDS; treat this as a blocked route like Jisc, not as a null. WHAT DID RESOLVE, VIA CROSSREF ON THE DISSERTATION TITLE REF-01005''S OWN BIBLIOGRAPHY CARRIES: John A. Templer''s indexed corpus is stairs, end to end -- ''An analysis of the behavior of stair users'', John A Templer, Gary M Mullet, John Archea, Stephen T Margulis, 1978, NBS internal report, DOI 10.6028/nbs.ir.78-1554; ''The Forgiving Stair'', 1984, Human Factors Society; ''The Soft Stair: Falls Induced by a Laboratory Stair'', 1989. Together with the 1974 dissertation ''Stair shape and human movement'' that REF-01005''s bibliography actually lists, that is FOUR Templer items spanning fifteen years and NOT ONE OF THEM IS ABOUT RAMPS, and not one is dated 1977. P3e ANSWERED IN AN UNEXPECTED DIRECTION: the ambiguity is not between several 1977 Templers, it is that there is no 1977 Templer at all and the nearest neighbours are 1974 and 1978. STILL NOT GUESSED, PER THE ASSIGNMENT: the 1978 NBS report is one year off and would be a plausible preprint-dated citation, and the 1974 dissertation is the one the report itself lists. Either could be footnote c''s referent and this pass does not choose. THE STRUCTURAL FINDING IS INDEPENDENT OF WHICH ONE IT IS: Table 13''s footnote c warrants the 1:8 and 1:10 rampway bands on two named parties, and one of them has no ramp research to his name. || CORRECTED 2026-09-20: RETRACTED: THE UNIVERSAL NEGATIVE IN THIS NOTE IS FALSE. This pass concluded that "John A. Templer corpus is stairs, end to end", "FOUR ITEMS ACROSS FIFTEEN YEARS AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS ABOUT RAMPS", and that "one of them has no ramp research to his name". Found false by an adversarial pass over this same batch, and the route that finds it is one this session used the same hour for a different target. TEMPLER HAS SUBSTANTIAL RAMP RESEARCH: Templer, John A., Provisions for elderly and handicapped pedestrians, FHWA-RD-79-1 (Jan 1979), -2 and -3 (May 1980), Georgia Institute of Technology Pedestrian Research Laboratory, contract DOT-FH-11-8504, OCLC 5844243, full text on Internet Archive. Volume 3 contains a Part I titled SHORT RAMPS, TACTILE SURFACES AND WHEELCHAIR DIMENSIONS opening with "A Study of Short Ramps"; 32 gradient-rating tables (manual wheelchair users, electric wheelchair users, cane users, ambulant disabled, able-bodied, ascent and descent); and TABLE 20, "Ramp Gradient Recommendations", recommending against curb heights of 3, 6 and 9 inches across gradients 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16. Derive it: curl the archive.org advancedsearch endpoint for creator:Templer AND (pedestrian OR ramp OR handicapped) -- 3 results -- then count ramp in provisionsforeld00temp_0_djvu.txt, which returns 998. HOW THE ERROR WAS MADE, because the shape matters more than the fact. (1) THE QUERY THAT "RESOLVED HIS CORPUS" CONTAINED THE WORD STAIR. It was query.bibliographic=Templer+stair+shape+human+movement. A query containing stair returning stair papers is the query shape, not the corpus -- which is R14, the rule this same note invokes against ERIC two sentences earlier, applied to everyone except myself. (2) archive.org was never pointed at Templer, though this batch used exactly that endpoint for Walter at 03:10. (3) ERIC returned numFound 33 and the request carried rows=25, so 25 docs were examined and EIGHT WERE NEVER SEEN; "33 records, none architectural" overstates what was looked at, and results_screened=43 is a hand-typed figure the artefact contradicts. WHAT THIS DOES TO THE BATCH CONCLUSIONS: candidate 115 correction stands as far as it goes (the bibliography Templer is the 1974 Columbia stairs dissertation, and footnote c cites 1977) but its reasoning is void; candidate 117 stages the wrong neighbour; and the inference that "the priority of retrieving Templer drops" is INVERTED -- Table 20 recommends over 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16, the SAME FOUR BANDS as Steinfeld Table 13, and footnote c marks the two Steinfeld did not test. That is now the strongest available lead on what footnote c means. Candidate 125 stages it. STILL NOT GUESSED: Volume 3 is dated May 1980 and footnote c says 1977; the project field tests ran to Sep 1977, so an interim report of the same contract is plausible and unverified.' WHERE "exec_id" = 90;

-- search_candidates: 1 insert(s), 5 update(s)
INSERT INTO "search_candidates" ("candidate_id", "exec_id", "found_under_slug", "suggested_slug", "disposition", "title", "locator", "locator_status", "tier_guess", "harm_finding", "why_not_admitted", "notes", "created_by_session", "created_at", "resolved_ref_id") VALUES (125, 90, 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'PENDING-VERIFICATION', 'Templer, John A. et al. (1979-1980) — Provisions for elderly and handicapped pedestrians, FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3, Georgia Tech Pedestrian Research Laboratory', 'No DOI. FHWA-RD-79-1 (Jan 1979), -2 and -3 (May 1980); contract DOT-FH-11-8504; OCLC 5844243. FULL TEXT ON INTERNET ARCHIVE, free: identifiers provisionsforeld00temp, provisionsforeld00temp_0 (Vol 3), provisionsforeld00temp_1. Derive: archive.org/advancedsearch.php?q=creator:Templer AND (pedestrian OR ramp OR handicapped).', 'RESOLVED', 3, 0, 'NOT ADMITTED ONLY BECAUSE THIS BATCH FOUND IT AT ITS OWN END, BY BEING PROVED WRONG. It should be the next batch''s first admission. THIS IS THE ITEM BATCH 19 DECLARED DID NOT EXIST: the search that concluded ''John A. Templer''s corpus is stairs, end to end, not one about ramps'' is retracted on exec 90, and this row is what refutes it. WHY IT IS DIRECTLY ON PARAMETER 3: Volume 3''s Part I is ''SHORT RAMPS, TACTILE SURFACES AND WHEELCHAIR DIMENSIONS'', opening with ''A Study of Short Ramps''. It carries 32 gradient-rating tables -- manual wheelchair users, electric wheelchair users, cane users, ambulant disabled and able-bodied, ascent and descent separately -- and TABLE 20 ''Ramp Gradient Recommendations'', a recommend/not-recommend matrix over curb heights 3, 6 and 9 inches against gradients 1:8, 1:10, 1:12 and 1:16. Derive the scale: 998 occurrences of ''ramp'' and 56 of ''1:12'' in the Volume 3 OCR text.', 'IT MAY ANSWER TABLE 13 FOOTNOTE c, AND THE COINCIDENCE IS STRIKING RATHER THAN CONCLUSIVE. Steinfeld''s Table 13 declares four allowable bands -- 1:8, 1:10, 1:12, 1:16 -- and marks the first two ''Based on research of others (Templer, 1977 and Walters, 1971)''. Templer''s Table 20 makes recommendations over THE SAME FOUR GRADIENTS. NOT GUESSED, per batch 18 section 9 item 3: Volume 3 is dated May 1980 and footnote c says 1977, so this cannot be the cited item as printed; the contract''s field tests ran Jul 1976 to Sep 1977, which makes an INTERIM REPORT of the same project a plausible referent, and that is a hypothesis to test by retrieval rather than a conclusion. IT ALSO CARRIES WALTER 1971 SECOND-HAND, which bears on GAP-026: its literature review describes Walter''s adjustable laboratory ramp in detail -- 20 feet at gradients 1:8.6 to 1:16, 10 feet at 1:5.5 to 1:10, four-point rating scale -- so a document this project CAN retrieve reports the study it cannot. That is corroboration of Steinfeld''s account of Walter by an independent reader, not a substitute for Walter itself. AND IT CONTESTS CANDIDATE 110''s AUTHOR: its reference list cites the 1957 Illinois dissertation as ''Dixon, Charles E.'', where Steinfeld''s bibliography plainly prints ''Elmer, C.D.''. Two primary sources disagree; neither is corrected from the other. RETRIEVAL IS FREE AND THE ROUTE IS PROVEN: archive.org/download/provisionsforeld00temp_0/provisionsforeld00temp_0_djvu.txt. Unlike Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 this needs no document delivery.', 'session_2026-09-20-research-batch-19-pre1990-chain-steinfeld-walter-templer', '2026-09-20 04:09', NULL);
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source): STAYS PENDING AND ITS DESCRIPTION IS NOW CORROBORATED BY A PRIMARY SOURCE RATHER THAN BY A REFERENCE LIST. Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0. BUT REF-01005 page 163 characterises it directly: "Elmer (1957) found that a 1:8 ramp slope was maximum for wheelchair users. However, his sample was taken from wheelchair users at a pioneering rehabilitation-education center and the findings probably reflect the high standard of excellence in rehabilitation training that the subjects received as part of their program." SO THE 1957 FINDING IS 1:8, AND THE FIELD OWN 1979 ASSESSMENT IS THAT IT IS UNREPRESENTATIVE BECAUSE THE SAMPLE WAS ELITE-TRAINED. That is a selection-bias critique published eight years before the ADA, and it is itself first-class historical evidence about how the threshold literature was weighed. A WEB SEARCH SUMMARY ASSERTED THIS WAS A UNIVERSITY OF IOWA MASTER THESIS AND NONE OF ITS OWN RETURNED LINKS SUPPORTED THAT — the claim is discarded, not recorded (CLAUDE.md 5(c)). The institution is NOT established: REF-01005 says only "a pioneering rehabilitation-education center" and naming one would be invention. || RESOLVED 2026-09-19 (R15, re-described from the source): RETRACTION OF THIS ROW OWN RETRIEVAL CLAIM. The re-description above states "Not retrieved: Open Library 0, Google Books 0, Internet Archive 0, ERIC 0". GOOGLE BOOKS 0 IS FALSE. That query returned HTTP 429 (daily quota exceeded) and was never asked; recording an unfired query as a null is the error batch 17 made on SafetyLit and is corrected here rather than left standing. THE OTHER THREE ZEROS HOLD and were re-derived, not assumed: Open Library numFound 0, Internet Archive 0 relevant, ERIC 0. EVERYTHING ELSE IN THE ROW IS UNAFFECTED — Elmer 1957 finding of 1:8 maximum, and Steinfeld 1979 dismissal of it as an elite-trained sample, come from REF-01005 page 163 and stand on a retrieved primary source. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 RE-DESCRIPTION FROM A PRIMARY SOURCE, AND THE ROW GAINS AN AUTHOR IT NEVER HAD. Staged from REF-00980''s Crossref reference list as a bare title with a year, ''A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps (1957)''. REF-01005 supplies three things the reference list did not, from two different places in the same document. (1) THE AUTHOR AND THE FORM, from its bibliography at pypdf page index 167: ''Elmer, C.D. [A s]t[udy] to determine the s[p]e[ci]fications of wheelchai[r] ramp[s]. Ph.D. d[is]ser[ta]tion, University o[f] Il[linois]''. NOTE HONESTLY: the bibliography''s own year digits are OCR-damaged and read ''194-5/'', which is NOT 1957 and is not resolvable from those bytes. (2) THE YEAR, from the body text at page index 162, which reads ''Elmer (1957)'' cleanly -- so 1957 is corroborated by a primary source, but by the prose, not by the reference entry. (3) THE FINDING, which is why this row now matters more than it did: REF-01005 reports that ''Elmer (1957) found that a 1:8 ramp slope was maximum for wheelchair users'' and then DISCOUNTS IT ON SAMPLING -- his subjects were young, highly trained users at a pioneering rehabilitation-education centre, where Walter''s sample and Steinfeld''s own included large proportions of older people and many with reduced arm function and low stamina. Captured as extraction 57 with contested=1. WHAT THIS CHANGES ABOUT THE ROW''S VALUE: staged as a hypothesis that the oldest ramp-specification study might be the origin of a threshold still in force. It is not that. Its figure is 1:8, which no code carries, and the 1979 report set it aside forty-seven years ago for a reason this project would give today. THE HISTORICAL TRAJECTORY IS THE FINDING: 1:8 (1957, discounted as unrepresentative) -> 1:12 (ANSI, measured and found wanting in 1979) -> 1:16 (Walter 1971 and Steinfeld 1979 converge) -> 1:20 (every wheelchair user in Steinfeld''s sample completed 40 feet). THE PARAMETER HAS MOVED IN ONE DIRECTION, GENTLER, FOR SEVENTY YEARS, AND EACH STEP WAS DRIVEN BY WIDENING THE SAMPLE. NOT RETRIEVED AND STILL OWED as a document: a 1957 Illinois PhD dissertation. ProQuest Dissertations and the University of Illinois institutional repository are the routes and neither has been tried. NOT AN ANCHOR IF RETRIEVED: owner ruling 2026-09-18 places pre-1990 work as historical grounding. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION, TWO PARTS, ONE OF WHICH OPENS A CONTEST THIS ROW MUST NOT CLOSE. (1) THE YEAR WAS ALWAYS PRINTED. My earlier re-description said the bibliography year digits read "194-5/" and were "NOT resolvable to 1957", and that 1957 came only from the body text. The rendered page reads "Elmer, C.D. A study to determine the specifications of wheelchair ramps. Ph.D. dissertation, University of IL, 1957." Author, form, institution and year are all printed in the reference entry. The elaborate distinction I drew between what the bibliography could support and what the prose could support was an artefact of reading a damaged text layer instead of the page. (2) THE AUTHOR IS NOW CONTESTED BETWEEN TWO PRIMARY SOURCES, and this is new evidence rather than a correction of mine. Templer FHWA-RD-79-3 (candidate 125) cites the same 1957 Illinois dissertation as "DIXON, Charles E. A study to determine the Specifications of Wheelchair Ramps, Unpublished Ph.D. Dissertation, University of Illinois, Champagne, Urbana, 1957", and describes its method -- a campus questionnaire, then an adjustable laboratory ramp at 1:8.6 to 1:5, 61 subjects, 30 feet in a single run or two 15-foot runs with a landing. Steinfeld bibliography plainly prints Elmer, C.D. TWO 1979-80 FEDERAL REPORTS DISAGREE ON THE AUTHOR OF THE SAME DISSERTATION. Neither is corrected from the other and NEITHER IS PREFERRED HERE. My earlier note said this row "gains an author it never had"; it gains two, and that should not be read as settled. THE FINDING THE ROW CARRIES IS UNAFFECTED: whoever wrote it, REF-01005 reports its 1:8 result and discounts it on sampling, and FHWA independently reports 61 subjects who were university students, which is the same objection from a second reader. RESOLUTION: ProQuest Dissertations or the University of Illinois repository, searched on TITLE rather than author, since the author is exactly what is in doubt.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 110;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'HYPOTHESIS, per R15 — this description is a guess until the item is read. REF-01005 Table 13 (Maximum Lengths and Slopes for Rampways) carries footnote c: "Based on research of others (Templer, 1977 and Walter, 1971)." SO THE HUD REPORT RECOMMENDATION TABLE IS NOT FOUNDED ON ITS OWN RAMP EXPERIMENT ALONE, which is the most important structural fact this batch found about the provenance chain: the numbers that reached ANSI A117.1 came from a synthesis of three studies, only one of which this project now holds. REF-00980 deposited reference list separately holds Templer J A (1984), "Guidelines for accommodating the handicapped on crossings: final report" — a DIFFERENT item, possibly a successor. WHICH TEMPLER IS LOAD-BEARING IS UNRESOLVED AND MUST NOT BE GUESSED. Nothing in any payload this session holds names Templer institution or other work, so none is recorded. Routes to try: ERIC, NTIS, and the TRID index that already held REF-01002. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION OF AN OVER-CLAIM IN THIS ROW''S OWN TITLE. The row was staged as ''Templer J A (1977) -- the ramp-slope research REF-01005 Table 13 names as one of its two warrants''. CALLING IT RAMP-SLOPE RESEARCH WAS A GUESS AND IT LOOKS WRONG. Two findings, neither of which resolves the item, and the assignment''s instruction that which Templer is load-bearing MUST NOT BE GUESSED is held to. (1) TEMPLER 1977 IS NOT IN REF-01005''S OWN BIBLIOGRAPHY. Table 13 footnote c cites ''Templer, 1977''; the reference list, transcribed this batch from pypdf page index 169, carries ''Templer, J.A. Stair sha[p]e and h[u]man movement, Unpublished doct[o]ral [dis]sertation. New [York]: [?] [Un]iversity, 1974.'' The year digits 1974 are clean in the OCR. So the report cites a year its own reference list does not carry. (2) NO TEMPLER 1977 EXISTS IN ANY INDEX REACHED, and John A. Templer''s corpus is STAIRS. Derive it: ERIC author:Templer returns 33 records, none architectural; ERIC Templer AND stairs returns 0; OpenAlex Templer + 1977 returns 10, none architectural; Crossref on the dissertation title returns his real corpus -- ''An analysis of the behavior of stair users'' (Templer, Mullet, Archea, Margulis, 1978, NBS internal report, DOI 10.6028/nbs.ir.78-1554), ''The Forgiving Stair'' (1984), ''The Soft Stair'' (1989). FOUR ITEMS ACROSS FIFTEEN YEARS, NOT ONE ABOUT RAMPS. WHAT THE ROW NOW ASSERTS, AND ONLY THIS: footnote c names a Templer as one of two warrants for Table 13''s 1:8 and 1:10 bands; the referent is unidentified; the two nearest candidates are the 1974 dissertation the report itself lists and the 1978 NBS report one year the other side, and THIS PASS DOES NOT CHOOSE BETWEEN THEM. WHY IT STILL MATTERS IF IT IS NEVER IDENTIFIED: whichever item footnote c meant, the person it names has no ramp research, so the warrant for the steepest rampway bands leans on stair-user behaviour. SCOPE CORRECTION THAT CUTS THE OTHER WAY AND REDUCES THIS ROW''S PRIORITY: extraction 56 records that footnote c''s marker appears on the 1:8 and 1:10 rows ONLY, not on 1:12 or 1:16 -- the bands Steinfeld actually tested. So Templer warrants the permissive end of the table, which no determination would rest on. GAP-037 treats retrieving Templer as one of two remaining nodes of the 1:12 chain; on this reading it is not part of that chain at all. This row stays PENDING-VERIFICATION rather than being retired, because a cleaner scan of page 57 could overturn the footnote-scope reading and put it back. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 SECOND CORRECTION, RETRACTING THE REASONING OF THE FIRST. My earlier re-description said Templer corpus is stairs end to end and that "the person it names has no ramp research". THAT IS FALSE and is retracted. Templer, John A., Provisions for elderly and handicapped pedestrians, FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3 (Georgia Tech Pedestrian Research Laboratory) is a dedicated ramp study: Volume 3 Part I is "SHORT RAMPS, TACTILE SURFACES AND WHEELCHAIR DIMENSIONS", with 32 gradient-rating tables and Table 20 "Ramp Gradient Recommendations" over 1:8, 1:10, 1:12, 1:16. Staged as candidate 125; full text free on Internet Archive. WHAT STILL STANDS, narrowly: this bibliography entry is Templer J.A., Stair shape and human movement, doctoral dissertation, New York: COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY, 1974 -- read off the rendered page, where my first pass wrote "[?] [Un]iversity" -- and Table 13 footnote c cites "Templer, 1977", so the report cites a year its own reference list does not carry. That discrepancy is real. THE CONCLUSION DRAWN FROM IT WAS NOT. I also wrote that the footnote scope "reduces this row priority" and that Templer is not part of the 1:12 chain. Table 20 recommends over the SAME FOUR BANDS as Steinfeld Table 13, and footnote c marks the two Steinfeld did not test -- so the FHWA work is now the strongest lead on what footnote c means, and the priority goes UP. THE 1978 NBS REPORT IS NO LONGER THE NEAREST NEIGHBOUR: candidate 117 was staged on that basis and should be read as superseded by 125 for this purpose. STILL NOT GUESSED: FHWA Volume 3 is dated May 1980 against footnote c 1977; the contract field tests ran to September 1977, so an interim report of the same project is a hypothesis to test by retrieval.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 115;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'Surfaced by the REF-01005 backward pass (exec 88). Authors as OCR''d: "Corle.-tt;t:N:'',-Huichesy :,e.;,-:Det.,Utill,,,L,M..and 6goiens4k.1" -- read as Corlett, [E].N., Hu[t]ches[on], [C]., De[L]u[gan], L.M. and [Ro]go[z]ens[ki], [J]. EVERY ONE OF THOSE SURNAMES AFTER THE FIRST IS RECONSTRUCTED FROM DAMAGED CHARACTERS AND NONE IS CONFIRMED. CLAUDE.md 5(c) forbids supplying a bibliographic field from memory when a payload is in hand, so they are recorded as read, with the damage visible, and not repaired. RESOLUTION ROUTE: Applied Ergonomics volume 3 is 1972 and IS indexed -- Crossref or ScienceDirect by journal plus volume plus title fragment should settle author list, exact title and pages in one retrieval. That is a cheap, well-formed query and it is owed. VERBATIM OCR PRESERVED in the derived artefact for this pass (retrieval-log/<session>/08263082ebdda8eb.json, entry with alpha_position C). || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION: THE TITLE DOES CONTAIN "Ramps", AND THIS ROW SAID IT MIGHT NOT. Staged from a damaged OCR text layer with the caution "THE TITLE MAY NOT CONTAIN Ramps AT ALL" and scored ceiling-only on every screen for that reason. The page renders legibly and reads: "Corlett, E.N., Hutcheson, C., DeLugan, M.A. and Rogozenski, J. Ramps or stairs, in Applied ergonomics, 3:4, pp 195-201, 1972." Every surname, the volume, the issue, the pages and the year are printed. CONSEQUENCE FOR A MEASUREMENT: this entry now scores in the FLOOR of slope-strict, so REF-01005 backward yield is 2, not the 1-2 band first reported -- and that band was measuring my own guesswork, not the document. RESOLUTION IS NOW CHEAP AND WELL-FORMED: Applied Ergonomics 3:4 (1972) is indexed; a Crossref query on journal, volume, issue and pages settles the DOI in one call. It was not run here only because this batch had already spent its retrieval budget being wrong about Templer.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 116;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'SURFACED WHILE FAILING TO FIND SOMETHING ELSE, which is the honest provenance. Crossref, queried on the dissertation title REF-01005''s bibliography carries, returned John A. Templer''s indexed corpus instead: this 1978 NBS report, ''The Forgiving Stair'' (1984, Human Factors Society, doi 10.1177/154193128402800118), and ''The Soft Stair: Falls Induced by a Laboratory Stair'' (1989, doi 10.1518/107118189786758148). Full author list here: John A Templer, Gary M Mullet, John Archea, Stephen T Margulis. WHAT MAKES IT CHEAP TO RESOLVE: NBS is now NIST and its internal reports are public, so unlike Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 this one is very likely retrievable in full without document delivery -- nvlpubs.nist.gov is the route and it has not been tried. IF RETRIEVED, THE QUESTION TO ASK IS NARROW: does it contain any rampway slope or length recommendation at all? If yes, footnote c probably means this and the 1:8/1:10 warrant is identified. If no -- which is what its title predicts -- then footnote c''s Templer is the 1974 dissertation or an item outside every index reached, and either way the warrant for those two bands rests on stair research. THAT QUESTION IS WORTH ONE RETRIEVAL AND NOT MORE. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION: THE PREMISE FOR STAGING THIS ROW IS GONE. It was staged as "the nearest RESOLVABLE item to Table 13 footnote c" on the reasoning that John A. Templer had no ramp research and that a 1978 stairs report was therefore the closest thing to the cited 1977 item. That reasoning is retracted on exec 90: Templer FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3 is dedicated ramp research and is candidate 125. THIS ROW IS NOW WHAT IT ALWAYS WAS ON ITS FACE -- a study of STAIR users, off parameter 3, with no claim to make about ramp gradient. Keep it PENDING rather than retiring it: NBS IR 78-1554 is also free on Internet Archive (identifier analysisofbehavi7815temp), it is by the same author in the same period, and if the FHWA trail fails to identify footnote c referent this is still a node worth reading. But it is no longer the first call, and the question this row was staged to answer should be put to candidate 125 instead.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 117;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'Surfaced by the query-shape lesson recorded in GAP-041: searching Open Library by PUBLISHER rather than title returns 99 DLF records, and these Felix Walter titles are among them. REF-01005''s bibliography reads its Walter entry as ''Wal ter,rE.'' -- an initial that is NOT an F on its face -- so an independent trail of Felix Walter DLF architectural titles is worth holding while that initial stays unexplained. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION: THE INITIAL THIS ROW EXISTS TO EXPLAIN WAS NEVER UNEXPLAINED. Staged partly because REF-01005 bibliography appeared to read "Wal ter,rE." and I recorded that the initial was "NOT an F on its face", making a second Felix Walter DLF title useful as corroboration. The page renders legibly: "Walter, F. Four architectural movement studies for the wheelchair and ambulant disabled. London: Disabled Living Foundation, 1971." THE INITIAL IS F AND THE YEAR IS 1971, printed plainly, and they agree with the Library of Congress MARC record already held (Felix Walter, DLF, 1971) and with Table 13 footnote c. There was nothing to corroborate. WHY THE ROW STAYS ANYWAY, on its own merits rather than the ones it was staged on: An introduction to domestic design for the disabled (Felix Walter, DLF, 1968) is a second built-environment title by the author of candidate 109, in the same series of DLF architectural work, and if Walter 1971 is ever obtained through document delivery its sibling is likely held by the same libraries. That is a retrieval convenience, not evidence, and this row should not be counted as anything more.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 120;

